ll days  of deliberations uh adding up to some 16  hours of deliberations so they have been  keeping busy during that short time  frame they have been requesting the  transcripts of multiple alleged victims  and as it turns out as of right before  the closing of deliberations yesterday  they requested all four accusing  witnesses they got the fourth that was  uh kate the woman testifying under that  pseudonym  and they had previously requested the  transcripts of the testimony of annie  farmer  jane and carolyn  and so with that the jury will reconvene  on monday bernardo let me start with you  any concern here with the jury taking  this break in the middle of  deliberations and then coming back to  finish the case  i'm not concerned just because it's a  holiday so you got to think with the  holidays people aren't really focused as  to deliberating because look for example  the ken potter jury they're sequestered  and they came back with a verdict  because they know it's christmas same  thing when we did the uh there was  another case that it was doing  thanksgiving the mod aubry case and they  came back right the day before  thanksgiving so in terms of this jury  they heard so much testimony and there's  so much that they need to think about  that they themselves were the ones that  requested no we do not want to come in  to continue deliberating that we request  to come in on monday instead  getting into the substance of terry and  these jury questions with the jury  taking such a close look at the  accuser's testimony if you were the  prosecution would you be nervous right  now  i would be nervous if i were the  prosecution actually i think both  parties should be nervous we understand  that after one of the questions asking  about the testimony from reports the  defense attorneys were giving each other  high fives i think that's too early to  celebrate you never know how this is  going to go they're looking at the  detailed testimonies of annie farmer  carol and jane and kate all four of  those accusers and there were some  inconsistencies carolyn as we mentioned  basically there was an inconsistency in  a 2007 interview with the fbi there were  also inconsistencies with jane in her  interviews in 2019 and 2020 so i think  the jury is taking a careful look and  they're going to determine whether or  not their testimony was credible and i  think it could go either way at this  point and neither party should be  celebrating while these jurors are  asking questions  all right maybe it's still early to  predict what will happen galen maxwell  but the jury will get some rest then  they'll continue their deliberations and  we'll follow what happens next  when we come back investigators are  hoping that newer technology will bring  new  new answers to a case that has gone cold  for quite some time coming up next the  announcement on what could help solve a  25 year old case i'm talking of course  about the murder of jonbenet ramsey  we'll be back  [Music]  and welcome back to long crime daily  everybody nearing the 25th anniversary  of the murder of jonbenet ramsey the  boulder colorado police department has  announced that it may still use dna  technology to help solve this cold case  the six-year-old's murder shocked the  nation back in 1996 when her body was  found in the basement of her family home  just one day after christmas ramsay's  parents reported her missing saying that  a ransom note had been left behind she  was later found bludgeoned and strangled  ramsey's family was ruled out as  suspects and no one was ever charged in  the case in a statement boulder police  said it has reviewed more than 1500  pieces of evidence related to the case  and followed up with more than 21 000  tips the department says it quote  continues to use new technology to  enhance the investigation now that it  regularly checks dna evidence for new  matches now bernardo we are no strangers  to dna solving cold cases but these this  kind of dna it has its advantages and it  has its disadvantages walk us through it  so in regards to this specific type of  dna that they're trying to look into  it's more of now they want to look into  genetic dna in terms of to see if they  can find someone where there's a  familiar match and once they find  someone within the familiar match then  they can start looking deeper into what  are the connections within the family  what is the family tree to see whether  there will be a positive hit as to that  dna i'll tell you this dna has been  known to crack cases open i remember the  first homicide that i tried as a  homicide prosecutor at the brooklyn da's  office it was a cold case from 20 years  prior where dna did not exist and it was  dna that cracked the case in terms of  that the person had the deceased had the  dna skin cells of the perpetrator  underneath her nails and it was the  coming of dna that helped open up that  case and solve it  terry you know what i find odd though is  that they kind of made a similar  announcement of this back in 2016. why  are they announcing this now this dna  technology when they haven't even  identified a suspect it feels like  something's happening behind the scenes  i agree i think what they are doing here  the authorities want the public to know  that they're doing something here and  that they didn't drop the ball remember  this was such a huge case back at the  time it was all over the news and the  authorities were focusing on the parents  and the parents claimed then that they  were focusing on the wrong people that  they weren't following credible leads  and so because of that this is part of  the reason why the case went cold this  is part of the reason why they weren't  able to solve the case so i think the  authorities are trying to say look we  are following this up we want you to  know we have not dropped the ball and  it's a message to the parents it's a  message to the community that they are  continuing to follow up on this dna even  though they don't have a suspect and the  other reason jesse is when they make  these types of announcements they always  give a telephone number to contact they  always say what they are doing because  they want the public to contact them and  tell them about more leads if possible  so it does help them to solve the case  to continuously keep it in the public  eye even though it's 25 years old yeah  it is such an eerie case and still gives  people shivers so if they can find  answers that would be incredible  bernarda terry thank you so much  everyone out there thanks for joining us  here on long crime daily we'll see you  next time as we discuss justice in  america  [Music]  you
 

